# A container run could not tell a tree it had read from one it had not (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` fingerprints the checkout before the first gate and again after
the last one, and the verdict line it prints at the end is attributed to that pair. Until this
batch, neither half of the fingerprint could report that it had failed, and the line that
reports whether the tree matches a commit read `git status`' output while discarding its exit
status.

Five shapes were measured against both revisions of the script: the same broken repository run
twice, once with `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` at `36d0809e` and once with the version in this
batch. Four of the five ended the run without a verdict at all -- silently, with nothing
printed about why. The fifth printed a full green verdict over a repository whose listing was
empty, which is the case where the run said something false rather than nothing.

Every transcript below is copied from those paired runs. Each repository was built by the
shipped fixtures (`scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py`), so the reproduction is the fixture
suite rather than a hand-built directory.

== 0. The two lines, before and after

At `36d0809e`, `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:149-160`:

    fingerprint() { # fingerprint <output-file>
      ( cd "${repo_root}" && git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 cksum ) >"$1"
    }

    sum_of() { cksum <"$1" | cut -d' ' -f1; }

    fingerprint "${tree_before}"
    echo "== source tree: $(grep -c '' "${tree_before}") files, checksum $(sum_of "${tree_before}")"
    dirty="$(cd "${repo_root}" && git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | grep -c '' || true)"
    if [ -n "${dirty}" ] && [ "${dirty}" != "0" ]; then
      echo "   ${dirty} path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit"
    fi

Three properties of that shape matter together. `fingerprint` has no exit status of its own:
the function body is one pipeline, so what it returns is whatever the pipeline returns, and
nothing at the call site looked. `2>/dev/null` on `git status` throws away the only text that
would explain a refusal. And `| grep -c ''` turns whatever happened before it into a number --
including a refusal, whose count of zero is the sentence a clean tree earns.

== 1. The same five broken repositories, run twice

The driver builds each repository, then runs the fixture's copy of the entry point at each
revision with `--only "tree ownership"`, so one gate runs and the only difference is the
script. `stub-image:tag` is the fixture's `docker` stub, which records every invocation; `|`
lines are its stdout and `!` lines its stderr, exactly as the driver captured them.

### A. `git` refuses the repository

Reachable without any contrivance: `detected dubious ownership in repository` is the reason
the container side of this same script carries a `safe.directory` bootstrap. The fixture
reproduces the exit status and the stderr, which is all the script can see.

    --- HEAD 36d0809e: exit=128
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at "/src"
    --- with the fix: exit=1
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at "/src"
        ! fingerprint: git ls-files -co --exclude-standard exited 128 after naming 0 path(s) in /tmp/tmp8d1k0a3v/repo
        !    a fingerprint over too few paths compares equal to the same too-few paths taken
        !    after the gates, so a verdict from this run could not be attributed to a commit.
        !    Is /tmp/tmp8d1k0a3v/repo a repository git will read?

At `36d0809e` the run stops at exit 128 with nothing of its own to say: `git`'s message is on
stderr, but the exit status is `git`'s, propagated through a function that never looked at it.
The image-build lines above it are the last thing a reader sees before the `fatal:`, and they
succeeded.

### B. `git` is healthy and the listing comes back empty

The tree's own ignore rules cover everything, and the index has been emptied, so `git ls-files
-co --exclude-standard` legitimately reports no paths and exits 0.

    --- HEAD 36d0809e: exit=0
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        | == source tree: 1 files, checksum 3871339299
        |    5 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
        | == --only is in effect: 1 of 36 gates selected, this is not a sweep
        | 
        | == preflight: container can read the repository
        | preflight: OK
        | 
        | == tree ownership
        | stub gate ran: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py
        | 
        | selected gates passed in stub-image:tag
        |   --only was in effect: 1 of 36 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep
    --- with the fix: exit=1
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! fingerprint: git ls-files -co --exclude-standard exited 0 after naming 0 path(s) in /tmp/tmptf6yygye/repo
        !    a fingerprint over too few paths compares equal to the same too-few paths taken
        !    after the gates, so a verdict from this run could not be attributed to a commit.
        !    Is /tmp/tmptf6yygye/repo a repository git will read?

This is the one that prints something false rather than nothing. `== source tree: 1 files` is
not one file's worth of fingerprint: the sums file is one line because GNU `xargs` runs its
command once on empty input, so `cksum` was handed no file arguments at all and read its own
standard input. The number on that line counts lines of the sums file, which is what both the
old `grep -c ''` and the new `count_lines` do, so it agrees perfectly with the number of
checksums taken and tells you nothing about the number of paths. The run then gates that tree
and reports `selected gates passed`, and the after-image is the same checksum of nothing, so
the movement check agrees with itself too.

The empty-listing check is also the only thing between this script and `xargs -0 cksum`
reading a terminal: on a developer's machine, with stdin a tty, that call blocks.

### C. `git` dies partway through the listing

A listing that stops after three of the repository's paths, with `git` exiting 128. No real
repository produces this on demand, which is why it is a stub `git` rather than a setup.

    --- HEAD 36d0809e: exit=128
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
    --- with the fix: exit=1
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! fingerprint: git ls-files -co --exclude-standard exited 128 after naming 3 path(s) in /tmp/tmpxcl2_dle/repo
        !    a fingerprint over too few paths compares equal to the same too-few paths taken
        !    after the gates, so a verdict from this run could not be attributed to a commit.
        !    Is /tmp/tmpxcl2_dle/repo a repository git will read?

`36d0809e` dies with no output at all, not even a failing command to point at. The fixed
version says how far the listing got, which is the part that decides whether the checksum is
short or merely late.

That count of paths had to be counted properly to be worth printing. The first version reused
`count_lines`, and the shipped fixture's listing contains a path with a newline in its name,
which is legal in git: GNU `grep -c ''` counts NUL-separated segments, so a newline inflated
the number the message exists to make trustworthy. `count_paths` counts NUL bytes instead,
which is what the shipped fixture's variant with the newline-bearing name pins.

### D. A path `git` lists and `cksum` cannot open

A tracked symlink whose target does not exist. A `chmod 000` file would not do as well:
`cksum` runs as root inside the container in the real deployment, and a broken link is
unreadable at any uid.

    --- HEAD 36d0809e: exit=123
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! cksum: dangling: No such file or directory
    --- with the fix: exit=1
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        ! cksum: dangling: No such file or directory
        ! fingerprint: cksum summed only 5 of 6 listed file(s) from /tmp/tmpstnpq4c3/repo (exit 123)
        !    a partial fingerprint is compared against the run's own after-image and agrees
        !    with it, so the verdict would name a commit this run did not actually read.

The sums file here is short rather than empty, which is the case a `-s` test cannot see. Both
images would be short in the same way, so the comparison the script exists to perform agrees
with itself about a tree it never finished reading.

### E. `git` refuses `status`

    --- HEAD 36d0809e: exit=0
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        | == source tree: 5 files, checksum 3305271461
        | == --only is in effect: 1 of 36 gates selected, this is not a sweep
        | 
        | == preflight: container can read the repository
        | preflight: OK
        | 
        | == tree ownership
        | stub gate ran: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py
        | 
        | selected gates passed in stub-image:tag
        |   --only was in effect: 1 of 36 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep
    --- with the fix: exit=0
        | == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
        | sha256:stubbed
        | == preparing cargo cache volumes
        | == source tree: /tmp/tmpkik3l2m8/repo at 0b37745: 5 files, checksum 1736566262
        |    git status exited 128, so this run cannot say whether the tree matches any
        |    commit: fatal: unable to read object database
        | == --only is in effect: 1 of 37 gates selected, this is not a sweep
        | 
        | == preflight: container can read the repository
        | preflight: OK
        | 
        | == tree ownership
        | stub gate ran: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-tree-ownership.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-tree-ownership.py
        | 
        | selected gates passed in stub-image:tag
        |   --only was in effect: 1 of 37 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

`36d0809e` prints the fingerprint, prints nothing about the tree's relationship to any commit,
and goes on to run the gates. An absent caveat line is read as a clean tree, because that is
exactly what the code writes for a clean tree: nothing. The fixed version prints the refusal
and `git`'s own first line, and still runs the gates, because a `git` that cannot do `status`
does not stop a container from checking code.

The `1 of 36` versus `1 of 37` difference is the same run's own gate count: this batch adds
the `Docker lab coverage` gate, recorded in
`docs/verification/docker-lab-coverage-2026-10-04.log`.

== 2. The fixtures, and which of them the old code failed

The 34-case suite was run against `36d0809e`'s copy of the script with everything else from
this batch in place -- the fixtures copy the entry point under test into a one-shot git
repository, so swapping that one file is the whole experiment:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py     # against scripts/verify-in-docker.sh at 36d0809e
    ERROR: test_a_file_nobody_committed_is_counted_and_declared (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_checkout_where_nothing_is_listed_stops_the_run (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_git_that_refuses_status_says_so_rather_than_implying_a_clean_tree (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_listed_file_that_cannot_be_read_stops_the_run (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_listing_git_died_partway_through_stops_the_run (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_repository_git_will_not_read_stops_the_run (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_tree_at_rest_prints_one_checksum_and_no_caveat (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_a_tree_with_no_commit_says_no_commit_rather_than_printing_nothing (__main__.VerdictTests)
    FAIL: test_the_fingerprint_line_says_which_checkout_it_describes (__main__.VerdictTests)
    Ran 34 tests in 2.745s
    FAILED (failures=8, errors=1)

Seven of the nine are the behaviour above: five assert an exit status and a message the old
code never produced (`AssertionError: 128 != 1`, `123 != 1`, `0 != 1` -- the `0 != 1` is case
B), and two assert the checkout and commit now named on the fingerprint line. Two are not
evidence about the old code and are labelled as such here:
`test_a_tree_at_rest_prints_one_checksum_and_no_caveat` and
`test_a_file_nobody_committed_is_counted_and_declared` parse that line with a regular
expression that expects the new fields, so they fail against the old wording whatever the old
code did. The ninth case that this batch added,
`test_a_second_checksum_that_fails_blames_the_tree_not_a_path`, passes at `36d0809e` as well:
the `|| fingerprint_ok=no` on the after-image call was already there, and this batch left it
alone.

The eleven mutants for this file, with the fixture that caught each, are in section 8 of
`docs/verification/docker-lab-coverage-2026-10-04.log`.

== 3. A mutation that could not be written

The driver was asked to kill one more mutant: drop `|| exit 1` from `fingerprint
"${tree_before}" || exit 1`, leaving the call bare. It survived, and the reason is that it is
not a mutant at all. The script runs under `set -e`, so a function call that returns non-zero
already ends the script at the same line, with the same output. The `|| exit 1` makes that
explicit for the reader and buys nothing observable.

The honest options were to keep a mutant that proves nothing, or to drop it and say so here.
It was dropped. What the fixture does prove is the pair a reader can actually rely on: the
message on stderr and the exit status of 1, asserted together, plus `self.fx.gate_runs() ==
[]`, which is the assertion that a run which cannot fingerprint itself runs no gate at all.

== 4. A checksum that cannot be reproduced, and what that says about the line

The `--full` container run of 2026-10-04 printed:

    == source tree: 4020 files, checksum 2692478763

Re-measuring that fingerprint today, in the very directory that run was launched from, which
is still at `36d0809e` and still clean:

    $ cd /var/tmp/labcov-full-36d0809e
    $ git log --oneline -1
    36d0809e fix(ci): `--only ""` 在两个 runner 上都不是过滤器，容器门禁入口第一次有自己的夹具
    $ git status --porcelain | wc -l
    0
    $ git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z | tr -dc '\0' | wc -c
    4020
    $ git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 cksum | cksum
    3045864519 303226

A fresh `file://` clone of `36d0809e` agrees with that second number, not with the printed
one: 4020 paths, `3045864519`. A third measurement the same day, a container run of the entry
point's own fixtures launched from a fresh clone of the same revision, printed the same
`4020 files, checksum 3045864519` on its way to its gate
(`docs/verification/container-entry-fixtures-2026-10-04.log`, section 8). So the count matches
and the checksum does not.

This is not explained, and it is stated rather than smoothed over. What can be said:

- The new failure modes would not have caught it. `git` exited 0, the listing was complete and
  non-empty, and every path was readable. Every check in section 1 passes on this tree.
- The difference is in contents, not in which paths were read, because the path count agrees.
- The listing was written to a file in a `mktemp -d` directory removed by the script's `EXIT`
  trap,
  so the per-path sums that produced `2692478763` no longer exist and cannot be diffed against
  today's.

What the line lacked was not a better checksum but an identity: it named neither the directory
it fingerprinted nor the commit it was taken at, so a transcript's number can only be
re-measured by guessing which of the several checkouts on this machine it came from. The
fingerprint line now carries both, which is what section 1's case E output shows (`== source
tree: /tmp/tmpkik3l2m8/repo at 0b37745: 5 files, checksum 1736566262`), and a repository whose
history has no commit prints `at (no commit)` rather than an empty field that reads as a
truncated sha. That is a small honesty fix, not a fix for the mismatch above; the mismatch
stays open here, and if it recurs on a run whose line now names its directory and its commit,
the next measurement has something to start from.

== 5. The gate written for this hazard fired on this batch's own new line

Section 4's `scripts/verify-gates.sh` sweep was red, and the single failing gate was about the
batch's own code. The `pipefail report` gate exists for the `grep -c ''` class of bug that
this whole log is about. The `count_paths()` function added in this batch was an instance of
it:

    $ scripts/verify-gates.sh
    == gates extracted from /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
       host run: the ${bootstrap} prefix is dropped, build gates are skipped
       env: RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 (mirrors the container's --env; set it to override)
    PASS  toolchain matches the pin
    (… 17 PASS lines elided here)
    PASS  script portability
    FAIL  pipefail report (exit 1)
    (… the 17-test log elided here; the failing test's own message is quoted below)
    PASS  container hygiene
    (… 15 PASS lines and 4 SKIP lines elided here)

    FAILED gates: pipefail report
    35 gate(s) run, 4 skipped, 1 failed

The gate runs `test-check-pipefail-report.py && check-pipefail-report.py`, so what failed
inside that entry was the test's anti-vacuity case, which runs the checker over this
repository and asserts exit 0. Its assertion message carries the finding:

    FAIL: test_repository_is_clean_and_actually_scanned (__main__.PipefailReportTests)
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    Traceback (most recent call last):
      File "/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py", line 275, in test_repository_is_clean_and_actually_scanned
        self.assertEqual(proc.returncode, 0, f"stderr: {proc.stderr}")
    AssertionError: 1 != 0 : stderr: scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:155: bytes="$(tr -dc '\0' <"$1" | wc -c)"
        wc: exits non-zero when its input cannot be read
        fix: the non-zero status here is a normal answer, and a plain assignment hands it to `set -e`; use a command without that status (`sed '/^$/d'`, `awk 'NF { n += 1 } END { print n + 0 }'`) or absorb it explicitly with `|| true` so the handling below stays reachable
    pipefail-report: 1 assignment(s) that can end an errexit script before it prints its report

`count_paths()` is called from inside the branch of `fingerprint()` that explains a
fingerprint which could not be taken. Under `set -euo pipefail` a `wc -c` that cannot read its
input exits non-zero, and inside a bare assignment that status reaches `errexit`: the run ends
on the counting line and the explanation it was in the middle of producing is never printed.
The accounting path was the thing that could break and it could not survive breaking, which is
the same shape as `grep -c ''` printing `0` while exiting `1`. `count_lines()` had carried its
`|| true` since the day it was written; the new function did not. No fixture of the entry
point would have said so: with the `|| true` removed, all 34 of them still pass (`Ran 34 tests
in 3.296s` then `OK`), because the counting succeeds in every one of them and only an
unreadable input makes it exit non-zero. The repository-wide scan of the shape is what catches
it.

The fix is the two tokens the remedy line names, with the reason beside them in
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`. The line number differs between the two quotes above (155
there, 159 below) because the comment above the function grew between the two measurements.

To see it fire, take the `|| true` out of that line and re-run the checker. It costs about two
seconds and needs no container and no network. The restore keeps its own copy instead of using
`git checkout --`, because while a batch is still uncommitted the index holds the pre-fix
revision, which is what a checkout hands back; that happened once while these numbers were
being taken, and the saved copy is what caught it:

    $ cp scripts/verify-in-docker.sh /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh
    $ sed -i 's/ | wc -c || true)"/ | wc -c)"/' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py; echo "exit $?"
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:159: bytes="$(tr -dc '\0' <"$1" | wc -c)"
        wc: exits non-zero when its input cannot be read
        fix: the non-zero status here is a normal answer, and a plain assignment hands it to `set -e`; use a command without that status (`sed '/^$/d'`, `awk 'NF { n += 1 } END { print n + 0 }'`) or absorb it explicitly with `|| true` so the handling below stays reachable
    pipefail-report: 1 assignment(s) that can end an errexit script before it prints its report
    exit 1
    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 17 tests in 1.267s

    FAILED (failures=1)
    $ cp /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    $ cmp /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh && echo restored
    restored
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py
    pipefail-report: OK (31 shell script(s), 23 with `set -e` scanned, 0 hazard assignment(s))

With the two tokens back, the sweep that opened this section is green again:

    $ scripts/verify-gates.sh 2>&1 | tail -1
    all gates passed on the host (35 run, 4 skipped)

== 6. Reproduce it

The fixtures need `git`, `python3` and a `bash`, and no Docker and no network:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py
    (34 per-test "ok" lines elided; the timing line varies per run)
    Ran 34 tests in 3.196s

    OK

To see the paired old-versus-new behaviour, copy the entry point from `36d0809e` over the
working tree's and re-run the suite, then restore:

    $ cp scripts/verify-in-docker.sh /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh
    $ git show 36d0809e:scripts/verify-in-docker.sh >scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py      # 9 of 34 fail
    $ cp /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    $ cmp /var/tmp/keep-verify-in-docker.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh && echo restored

Against the real container the same line is printed by any run, and `--only` keeps it to one
gate:

    $ scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "Docker lab coverage"
